<title>UK: UK's Clarke reaffirms sympathy for idea of EMU.</title>
<headline>UK's Clarke reaffirms sympathy for idea of EMU.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1996-12-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>British Chancellor of the Exchequer Kenneth Clarke said he was sympathetic to the idea of economic and monetary union (EMU).</p>
<p>&quot;I have made it quite clear that I am sympathetic to the idea of economic and monetary union,&quot; he told parliament during a debate on European integration.  </p>
<p>Clarke defended Britain's option on whether to join a single currency or not.</p>
<p>&quot;I believe the government's policy of retaining the choice in both directions, and exercising it responsibly in the national interest, is what we're all about.&quot;</p>
<p>He said he regarded the timetable for a single currency, setting a start date of 1999, as the weakest part of the Maastricht Treaty.</p>
<p>-- Richard Murphy, London Newsroom 44 171 542 2774</p>
